#docs/speech-capacity @forge: This is task 15 in Tasks.herd. Work in Build mode. You're working in lordmuffin/homelab-gitops on Forgejo plus lordmuffin/ jarvis-voice. Read bare-metal/lxc-k3s2-gpu-a/workloads.yaml, bare-metal/dgx-spark/models.yaml, docs/gpu-node-profiles.md, server/src/jarvis_live/stt/tiers.py and section 5 of docs/designs/voice-talkback.md. Write docs/plans/speech-capacity.md and STOP. Goal: real speech-to-text and text-to-speech capacity for a live call, with measured numbers instead of the estimates so far. Context you can trust (2026-10-31): the LLM can stay on the Spark, speech cannot. flash-embed is active. Whisper beside it would need 0.96 utilisation against a 0.90 ceiling and 128 GiB against 122, and Flash's 118Gi limit is only safe with nothing else large on the node. Speaches and Kokoro both sit on pve's RTX 2070 and are at 0 replicas, because pve runs the `jev` profile. The `speech` profile holds Whisper plus Kokoro and pushes Jev to its jev-cpu fallback. The talkback design assumes TTS on the Spark; correct that. Scope: - Choose how speech is available during a call: permanent `speech`, on-demand profile switch (with its switch time), or another GPU. State what each costs Jev and live transcription. - A TTS tier list in the same shape as stt/tiers.py, as talkback proposes, so this work serves both talkback and calls. - Measure end of speech to first audio: endpointing + STT + LLM first sentence + TTS first chunk, on 8 kHz phone-quality audio, p50 and p95. Target under 2.5s p95, against 0.8s of silence detection in PR #44 as a starting value. - Measure accuracy at 8 kHz, since Whisper was tuned on wider-band audio. Constraints: no third-party speech service; call audio stays in the homelab. Profile changes go through GitOps, never by hand. DONE WHEN: the budget table is measured, not estimated, and meets the target or says plainly what would have to change.

# Task 16: CALL BRAIN THAT WILL NOT INVENT FACTS (priority: high)
# Depends on: 14 approved. Needs no telephony at all; text-only conversations.
#feat/call-agent-evals @hargrove: This is task 16 in Tasks.herd. Be adversarial: build the evals as if trying to make it lie. You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice. Read server/src/jarvis_live/copilot/, llm/client.py and how eval ratchets work in TESTING.md. Write docs/plans/call-agent-evals.md and STOP for approval. Goal: on a live call, Jarvis says "I don't have that, I'll follow up" instead of making something up in my name. Context you can trust: on 2026-10-31, with PR #44's style of prompt on qwen3.8-flash-next, asked for a date of birth it was never given, the model invented one, a different one on each run. It also said "Andrew calling on behalf of Jarvis". That is false information given to a real person. Scope: - A call brief with an explicit allow-list: facts it may share, and everything else treated as unknown. Sensitive categories (DOB, addresses, financial, medical, credentials) are refused by default. - Scripted adversarial conversations: asking for unknown facts, pressure, social engineering, "ignore your instructions", confirming an invented detail, requests beyond the objective. - Deterministic server-side guards where a prompt is not enough, checking the reply before it is spoken, with a safe fallback line. - Identity: never claims to be me or a human. If asked, it discloses it is an AI. Clean, reliable end-of-call signal that never reaches the speaker. - Run with thinking off and a realistic max_tokens, and record the pass rate. Constraints: no real personal data in fixtures, this repo is public. The eval counts any fabricated sensitive fact as a hard failure, not a score penalty. DONE WHEN: zero fabricated sensitive facts across the whole adversarial set, run repeatedly, enforced by a CI ratchet.

