# Task 1: ISOLATION TESTS, SOFT DELETE AND BACKUPS (#218)
#feat/isolation-and-backups: You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice. Read the repo first (README, CLAUDE.md, server/src/jarvis_live/{db,users,oidc,api,retention}.py, the alembic migrations, and the np-home-homelab manifests if reachable). Write a plan to docs/plans/isolation-and-backups.md and STOP for my approval before coding. Context you can trust, do not re-litigate: Pocket ID sign-in and per-user sessions already exist. There is no tenant column anywhere. There is no deleted_at anywhere. retention.py only unlinks *.pcm for sessions in status 'done' and keeps every row forever. There is no backup tooling at all. Goal: prove users cannot see each other's data, make deletes reversible, and make recordings recoverable after a disk loss. Scope: a) Isolation. Audit every read path -- session list, session detail, audio bytes, transcript, copilot output, final note, search, notify -- for a missing user filter. Add an integration test suite with 2 users that asserts 404/403 (not an empty 200) on every cross-user read and write. Note in the plan whether a tenant layer above users is worth adding now or whether user scoping is sufficient; recommend one. b) Soft delete. Add deleted_at to sessions. Delete hides the row and keeps audio and transcript. Add a "Recently deleted" list plus a restore endpoint for one session. Surface both in web/ and android/. c) Retention. Extend retention.py: after a configurable window (default 30 days) soft-deleted sessions are hard-deleted, rows and audio together. Keep the existing 90-day PCM purge for live sessions as its own setting. Allow "delete after N days" on a single session. d) Backups. Schedule a Postgres dump and an audio-dir backup. Document RPO and RTO. Write docs/runbooks/restore.md and an automated test that restores one specific soft-deleted session from a backup into a scratch namespace and asserts the audio bytes match. Constraints: no breaking API changes without an alembic migration; a test for every item; Gotify notification on backup failure. DONE WHEN: plan approved, server + web + android tests green in CI, restore runbook committed, one real restore performed and logged in the runbook.

# Task 6: LOCAL-ONLY ENFORCEMENT FOR WORK CALLS (#212, #214, #219)
#feat/local-only-audit: You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice. Read the Local-only path end to end before anything else: macos/, server/src/jarvis_live/{ingest,api/stream.py}, protocol/v1/. Write a findings report to docs/plans/local-only-audit.md and STOP. Context: the 10/09 handoff locked a per-session Local-only toggle -- draft transcript stays on the Mac, nothing uploads -- because streaming Inspire calls to homelab infra may conflict with employer data policy. I need to know that toggle is actually airtight, not just present in the UI. Scope: a) Audit. Trace every byte that leaves the client in local-only mode: audio frames, partial text, copilot requests, vault lookups, telemetry, crash logs, Gotify bodies, session titles. List anything that escapes. Be adversarial about it -- assume the toggle is wrong until proven. b) Enforce. Make local-only a hard gate at the transport layer, not a check at each call site, so a new feature cannot leak by forgetting to ask. c) Test. A test that runs a full local-only session against a network stub that FAILS the test on any outbound request other than the explicitly allowed set. d) Make it visible. The recording indicator states local-only vs streaming unambiguously, on macOS and Android. I should never have to guess which mode a running session is in. e) Default policy: if classification is ambiguous, default to local-only. Constraints: report findings before changing code. If the audit finds a real leak, say so bluntly in the report rather than quietly patching it. DONE WHEN: the no-egress test is green in CI and the report lists every allowed outbound call with a reason.

