#docs/llm-gateway-options @atlas: This is task 13 in Tasks.herd. If the probe from task 11 is not merged yet, finish the paper research and inventory, say the measured comparison is still outstanding, and stop. You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice. Output docs/plans/llm-gateway- options.md. This is research plus one measured comparison, not a migration. Goal: know whether a different gateway would actually help, on my hardware, with my traffic, so a swap is decided on evidence and not on a vendor's chart. Context you can trust: public comparisons found on 2026-10-31 are mostly written by vendors, and no independent like-for-like benchmark turned up. Bifrost's "54x faster" and similar figures come from its maker. LiteLLM's own published overhead is single-digit milliseconds, which does not describe a 5 second stall, so a stall that size is more likely a specific bug, config or load problem than inherent gateway overhead. Treat that as a hypothesis to test. Scope: - Inventory what the proxy does for me today before comparing anything: aliases and role routing, fallbacks and context-window fallbacks, the `auto` router and its CNPG decision log, the tool-call finish_reason fix, the qwen-shim, hosted_vllm, ollama, openrouter, audio transcription, embeddings, keyless LAN access, and every client that depends on each. - Candidates, at minimum: LiteLLM tuned, voice-only direct to vLLM behind a thin alias, Bifrost, Envoy AI Gateway, self-hosted Portkey, Kong AI Gateway. Drop any that fail a hard requirement, and say which and why. - Top two or three: run 11's probe on the same hardware, same traffic. - For each: migration cost, what breaks, licence and any paid-tier gating, and Grayfield's question: who gets paged and what is the failure mode at 3 AM. - Recommend, including "keep LiteLLM" if that is what the numbers say. Constraints: per Atlas, every claim cites a source, vendor numbers are labelled as vendor numbers, and thin evidence is stated. A direct-to-vLLM path for voice is a bypass of the all-calls-via-LiteLLM rule; it needs my explicit sign-off and loses fallbacks, so name that cost. DONE WHEN: Grayfield has reviewed it and I can make the decision from the doc.

